--------------------------------------------------------------------------
LICENCE OF THE NATIVE BINARIES (read before you redistribute)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MIT grant above covers the JuceSharp source in this repository: the
managed C# wrapper and the other managed projects, the examples, the tools,
the tests, and the native C++ source we wrote. It does NOT make the native
binaries MIT.

Every build of the native library statically links JUCE 9: `juce_sharp.dll`
(and `libjuce_sharp.so` / `.dylib` where built) links JUCE 9 and the Rubber
Band Library, and the sandboxed VST3 host `juce_vst_host.exe` links JUCE 9.
Such a build, and every
NuGet package or application that carries one, is therefore distributed under
the GNU Affero General Public License version 3 (AGPLv3, from JUCE 9) together
with the GNU GPL terms of Rubber Band (GPLv2 or later) and of the ASIO SDK
headers JUCE bundles (GPLv3), unless you hold commercial licences from JUCE
Ltd., Particular Programs Ltd. (Rubber Band) and Steinberg. In practice: if
you distribute the native library, you must offer its complete corresponding
source under the AGPLv3. This repository at the commit you built, plus the
pinned FetchContent dependencies its CMakeLists.txt downloads, is that source;
docs/getting-started/building-native.md says how to rebuild it.

Components linked into the native library (full notices, copyright lines and
licence texts: THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md):

* JUCE 9 (tag 9.0.3) -- AGPLv3 or the commercial JUCE 9 licence.
* Rubber Band Library (tag v4.0.0) -- GPLv2 or later, or a commercial licence.
* Steinberg VST 3 SDK 3.8.0 (bundled inside JUCE) -- MIT.
* Steinberg ASIO SDK 2.3 headers (bundled inside JUCE) -- GPLv3 or the
  proprietary Steinberg ASIO licence.
* ww2ogg's Vorbis codebooks and header rebuild -- BSD-style.
* NeuralAudio (MIT) with NAM Core (MIT), RTNeural (BSD-3-Clause), math_approx
  (BSD-3-Clause), xsimd (BSD-3-Clause), nlohmann/json (MIT) and Eigen
  (MPL-2.0) -- the neural amp capture module, on by default.
* demucs.cpp (MIT) with Eigen (MPL-2.0) -- only when built with
  -DDOTCHARTER_STEMS_DEMUCS_CPP=ON (off by default).

The managed packages also reference Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.DirectML (MIT)
and DirectML (Microsoft's proprietary redistributable licence); see
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md for those, the sample data compiled into the DLL, the
voice packs, and what the stem-separation backends download at run time.

`JuceSharp.Dsp` alone is plain MIT: it contains no native code and references
nothing.

This statement is informational and is not legal advice. Verify the current
terms with each vendor before you redistribute anything.
